<section class="nc-guide" id="guide">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>The default entries describe a man 70 inches tall with a 15-inch neck and a 34-inch waist. The Navy equation puts him at about 17.4% body fat, in the "fitness" band for men. The waist is the measurement that moves the result most: at a 32-inch waist the estimate is about 13.4%, and at 36 inches about 21.1%, roughly 2 percentage points per inch.</p>
    <p>That sensitivity is the method's strength and its weakness. A tape that slips half an inch changes the answer by about a point, so careful, consistent measuring matters more than the decimal places on the result.</p>

    <h2>How to measure</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Neck:</strong> just below the larynx (Adam's apple), with the tape angled slightly downward toward the front.</li>
      <li><strong>Waist:</strong> for men, horizontally at the navel; for women, at the narrowest point of the torso. Measure at the end of a normal breath out, without pulling the stomach in.</li>
      <li><strong>Hips</strong> (women only): around the widest part of the buttocks.</li>
      <li><strong>Height:</strong> without shoes.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Keep the tape level and snug against the skin without compressing it. Take each measurement 2 or 3 times and use the average. For tracking, measure at the same time of day, ideally in the morning before eating.</p>

    <h2>What the categories mean</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th class="num">Men</th><th class="num">Women</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Essential fat</td><td class="num">2–5%</td><td class="num">10–13%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Athletic</td><td class="num">6–13%</td><td class="num">14–20%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Fitness</td><td class="num">14–17%</td><td class="num">21–24%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Average</td><td class="num">18–24%</td><td class="num">25–31%</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Above average</td><td class="num">25% and up</td><td class="num">32% and up</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>These bands are widely used fitness-industry ranges, not medical diagnoses. Women carry more essential fat than men for reproductive and hormonal reasons, which is why every band is higher. Body fat that falls into the essential range is not a goal: very low levels can disrupt hormones and other body functions, and anyone concerned about weight or eating should talk with a clinician.</p>

    <h2>How accurate is the Navy method?</h2>
    <p>The equations were developed by the U.S. Navy from measurements of service members, then checked against underwater weighing. For groups of people they work reasonably well; for an individual, studies comparing tape methods with lab methods such as DEXA often find differences of several percentage points in either direction. People with unusual body shapes, very high or very low body fat, or a lot of muscle are the most likely to get misleading results.</p>
    <p>Use the estimate as a trend line. If your measurements are taken the same way each time, a waist that shrinks while your weight holds steady tells you something useful, even if the absolute percentage is off. For a precise number, a DEXA scan or air-displacement test (Bod Pod) is the standard.</p>
  </div>
  <section class="hub-faq" aria-labelledby="guide-faq-title"><h2 id="guide-faq-title">Body fat questions</h2>
    <details><summary>Is the Navy body fat method accurate?</summary><p>It is a reasonable estimate for most people but can differ from lab methods like DEXA by several percentage points for an individual. It is most useful for tracking change when measurements are taken the same way each time.</p></details>
    <details><summary>Where do I measure my waist for the Navy method?</summary><p>Men measure horizontally at the navel. Women measure at the narrowest point of the torso. Measure after a normal breath out without pulling the stomach in.</p></details>
    <details><summary>What is a normal body fat percentage?</summary><p>Common fitness ranges put average adults at about 18 to 24% for men and 25 to 31% for women. These are general ranges, not medical cutoffs.</p></details>
  </section>
